        "id": "p36-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "List the factors of the number 49",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1,7,49$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}49&=1\\times49\\\\49&=7\\times7\\end{align*}$$\nThe factors are $1,7,49$. Count $7$ once.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-q12-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "bffc7187-6796-4ef4-9ccb-f24142f55dc2",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 0,
              "lineHeight": 1.3199970000068182,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The number of positive factors of a positive square number will always be odd. ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "break"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Why is this true?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Positive factors occur in pairs, except the square root, which pairs with itself and is counted once. An even number of other positive factors plus one is odd.",
          "worked": "For a positive square number, pair each positive factor with the factor that multiplies with it to give the number. All pairs contain two different factors except the square root paired with itself. Count this factor once, giving an even number of other positive factors plus one, which is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-q12-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Without evaluating, decide which of these are perfect squares: $2^4\\times3^2$, $2^3\\times3^2$ and $2^2\\times3^4\\times5^2$. Explain, then state a rule.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The first and third. A number is a perfect square when every exponent in its prime factorisation is even.",
          "worked": "$2^4\\times3^2$ and $2^2\\times3^4\\times5^2$ are perfect squares: every exponent is even, so each prime can be shared equally into two identical groups, for example $2^4\\times3^2=(2^2\\times3)^2$. In $2^3\\times3^2$ the exponent of $2$ is odd, so it is not a perfect square. Rule: a positive integer greater than $1$ is a perfect square exactly when every exponent in its prime factorisation is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
